WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.767
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:08.525
convert Kerberos accounts to group and
delegated managed service accounts.

3
00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:11.557
This module is the
on-premises part of Gate 5,

4
00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:15.262
Conversion, with Chapter 6
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:19.706
You'll give every Kerberos
account a reviewed disposition,

6
00:00:19.706 --> 00:00:24.330
plan its move to a G M S A or, on a
Windows Server 2025 domain, a D M S A,

7
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.590
list what stays on the Kerberos island
with an S P N owner and an end date,

8
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.487
and reconcile every managed account
with the program's end state.

9
00:00:34.487 --> 00:00:38.387
Then you'll see the write path run
a plan only by its approved hash.

10
00:00:38.387 --> 00:00:43.550
The domain is fictional, and the
apply changes only a fake, in memory.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.450
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.963
Some workloads can't leave Kerberos yet.

13
00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:53.905
A traditional service
account with an S P N and a

14
00:00:53.905 --> 00:00:57.150
password that never expires
is the riskiest of them.

15
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:02.135
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
group managed service accounts says

16
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:06.575
a G M S A gives the same workload a
password that Windows changes for it.

17
00:01:06.575 --> 00:01:12.083
And Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts says a D M S A, on a

18
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:18.125
Windows Server 2025 domain, supersedes
the old account and disables its password.

19
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:22.435
But a G M S A works only while
Active Directory is authoritative,

20
00:01:22.435 --> 00:01:26.662
which is why the program's guidance
gives every one an end date.

21
00:01:26.662 --> 00:01:28.588
So converting isn't the end.

22
00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:34.185
Every G M S A and D M S A must either
move to a workload identity, retire,

23
00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:39.987
or sit on a dated Kerberos island list,
with an S P N owner and an end date.

24
00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.077
In this module you plan all three,

25
00:01:42.077 --> 00:01:45.990
and you see the write path
refuse a plan nobody approved.

26
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.578
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:54.965
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:54.965 --> 00:01:58.490
You'll name the four dispositions
a Kerberos account can have,

29
00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.803
and what each one needs.

30
00:01:59.803 --> 00:02:04.243
You'll explain why a D M S A can't be
used for a managed service account,

31
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:07.553
or for an account with
unconstrained delegation.

32
00:02:07.553 --> 00:02:12.553
You'll read the plan, the island list and
the reconciliation for a fictional domain.

33
00:02:12.553 --> 00:02:15.109
And you'll show that the
write path runs only a

34
00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:17.803
plan whose hash was
approved, and never deletes.

35
00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:24.350
Before you start, finish Module 1, because
the lab reads Chapter 1's register, and

36
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:30.393
Module 3, where the matrix sends Kerberos-only
accounts to a G M S A or a D M S A.

37
00:02:30.393 --> 00:02:35.228
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and a clone.

38
00:02:35.228 --> 00:02:36.500
No tenant.

39
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.075
Unit 1 is the guide's gate.

40
00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:47.493
Its Step 5 is the program's guidance:
build the target, give it two human owners,

41
00:02:47.493 --> 00:02:52.825
run old and new in parallel for thirty
days, then disable the legacy account.

42
00:02:52.825 --> 00:02:53.925
Never delete it.

43
00:02:53.925 --> 00:02:56.559
Step 2 adds the
prerequisites for a G M S A.

44
00:02:56.559 --> 00:03:01.826
Microsoft Learn's page on creating a K D S
root key says the key is created once per

45
00:03:01.826 --> 00:03:05.178
domain, and domain controllers
wait up to ten hours

46
00:03:05.178 --> 00:03:07.972
after it's created before
they allow a G M S A.

47
00:03:07.972 --> 00:03:12.601
Step 3's seventh step covers the
Active Directory side of credentials:

48
00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:18.347
Learn's guidance on securing group managed
service accounts says Windows changes the

49
00:03:18.347 --> 00:03:20.901
password every thirty days by default,

50
00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:24.812
and the interval can be set only
when the account is created.

51
00:03:24.812 --> 00:03:27.735
The rollback for Step 5 is
to re-enable the legacy

52
00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:30.950
account and revert the
application's configuration.

53
00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.667
A delegated account runs
the same thirty days:

54
00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:38.529
Learn's overview of delegated managed
service accounts recommends waiting four

55
00:03:38.529 --> 00:03:42.462
ticket lifetimes, and thirty days
covers four seven-day lifetimes,

56
00:03:42.462 --> 00:03:45.610
so the program completes
a migration only then.

57
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.560
Unit 2 starts with the
context the code depends on.

58
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.047
Every Kerberos account gets
one of four dispositions.

59
00:03:56.047 --> 00:03:58.724
Convert, to a G M S A or a D M S A,

60
00:03:58.724 --> 00:04:04.334
with the new account's name,
the workload and its hosts.

61
00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:06.568
Island: it stays on Kerberos,

62
00:04:06.568 --> 00:04:11.810
with an S P N owner and an end date
no later than decision D oh eight's.

63
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.760
Workload identity: Chapter 5 moves it.

64
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.622
Or retire, through the plan's
reviewed decommission process.

65
00:04:18.622 --> 00:04:21.147
The program adds four rules of its own.

66
00:04:21.147 --> 00:04:24.472
They're the program's design
guidance, not Microsoft's.

67
00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:27.816
Every Kerberos account has
a reviewed disposition,

68
00:04:27.816 --> 00:04:30.460
kept in a file the application owners own.

69
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.540
Who may retrieve a managed
password is reviewed data,

70
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.060
not whatever Active Directory holds today.

71
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.148
Every account that stays has an
S P N owner and an end date.

72
00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:43.900
And never delete: the old
account is disabled at most.

73
00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.712
The check runs nine rules, N
P I K R B oh one to oh nine.

74
00:04:52.712 --> 00:04:57.237
Oh one, oh four and oh eight come from
Microsoft Learn's pages on the root key,

75
00:04:57.237 --> 00:05:02.355
delegated managed service accounts and
securing group managed service accounts;

76
00:05:02.355 --> 00:05:05.099
the others are the
program's design guidance.

77
00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:07.236
Oh one: there's no K D S root key,

78
00:05:07.236 --> 00:05:10.950
or one that became usable
less than ten hours ago.

79
00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:15.700
Oh two: a Kerberos account has no
disposition, or an unknown one.

80
00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.711
Oh three: the accounts
allowed to retrieve a managed

81
00:05:18.711 --> 00:05:22.213
password in Active Directory
differ from the reviewed list.

82
00:05:22.213 --> 00:05:25.887
Oh four is the one to
remember: an unusable target.

83
00:05:25.887 --> 00:05:30.720
Learn's overview and F A Q for delegated
managed service accounts say a D M S A

84
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.370
can't come from a managed service account,

85
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.513
can't serve an account with unconstrained
delegation, which stops working once

86
00:05:38.513 --> 00:05:43.813
the migration completes, and needs Windows
Server 2025 on the hosts and the domain.

87
00:05:43.813 --> 00:05:48.259
Oh five and oh six: an island
account with no S P N owner,

88
00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:51.362
or no end date, or one after D oh eight's.

89
00:05:51.362 --> 00:05:54.963
Oh seven: an account not
reconciled with the end state.

90
00:05:54.963 --> 00:06:00.310
Oh eight warns about a privileged group,
and oh nine hands SQL Server accounts on.

91
00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:07.285
Each account becomes exactly
one action in the plan.

92
00:06:07.285 --> 00:06:09.026
The G M S A path: create it,

93
00:06:09.026 --> 00:06:13.423
once the root key is ready and
the retrievers are reviewed.

94
00:06:13.423 --> 00:06:17.685
Wait while the server owner installs
it and changes the service's identity.

95
00:06:17.685 --> 00:06:21.058
Then a thirty-day parallel
run, the program's guidance,

96
00:06:21.058 --> 00:06:23.435
and only then disable the old account.

97
00:06:23.435 --> 00:06:29.360
The D M S A path: create it, if it passes
every limit, and start the migration.

98
00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.410
After the thirty-day
parallel run, complete it.

99
00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:37.112
Microsoft Learn's overview of
delegated managed service accounts says

100
00:06:37.112 --> 00:06:41.423
completing disables the old account
and moves its S P N's to the D M S A.

101
00:06:41.423 --> 00:06:45.498
Set retrievers fixes a list that
differs from the reviewed one.

102
00:06:45.498 --> 00:06:48.851
Anything else, like island,
blocked or handed off,

103
00:06:48.851 --> 00:06:52.122
is recorded with its
reason, and writes nothing.

104
00:06:52.122 --> 00:06:54.531
The plan carries every input's hash,

105
00:06:54.531 --> 00:07:00.210
and the same inputs always give the same
bytes, so a reviewer approves one hash.

106
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.385
These are the kit's design.

107
00:07:05.385 --> 00:07:07.997
Two outputs carry the chapter's end date.

108
00:07:07.997 --> 00:07:13.339
The island list holds every account that
stays on Kerberos, with its S P N owner,

109
00:07:13.339 --> 00:07:17.011
its end date, and whether it's
ready for P four point six,

110
00:07:17.011 --> 00:07:19.348
the program's S P N retirement work.

111
00:07:19.348 --> 00:07:22.335
The hand-off is ready
only when every row is.

112
00:07:22.335 --> 00:07:26.517
The reconciliation covers
every G M S A and D M S A,

113
00:07:26.517 --> 00:07:30.485
existing or planned, and
says where it ends up.

114
00:07:30.485 --> 00:07:33.649
Writing to the domain
lives in a separate script,

115
00:07:33.649 --> 00:07:38.631
and it refuses to run unless the plan's
hash is the approved one, the plan is for

116
00:07:38.631 --> 00:07:41.399
this domain and no more
than seven days old,

117
00:07:41.399 --> 00:07:43.772
and it's a person's session, never a job.

118
00:07:43.772 --> 00:07:47.872
Nothing that removes, undoes
or resets is ever run.

119
00:07:47.872 --> 00:07:50.681
And the read holds no
domain administration:

120
00:07:50.681 --> 00:07:55.919
Microsoft Learn's page for the root key
class says its default security descriptor

121
00:07:55.919 --> 00:07:59.487
grants only Enterprise Admins,
Domain Admins and SYSTEM,

122
00:07:59.487 --> 00:08:04.860
so the read gets a delegated read of the
key's start and create times, nothing more.

123
00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:09.335
Try it.

124
00:08:09.335 --> 00:08:15.010
Lab 1 runs in the non-person identity
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

125
00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:19.973
Its step one makes Chapter 1's register,
so the chapter's steps are one ahead here.

126
00:08:19.973 --> 00:08:24.472
The inventory prints its three lines,
ending with where it wrote the register.

127
00:08:24.472 --> 00:08:28.047
Step two runs Microsoft
Learn's published gMSA,

128
00:08:28.047 --> 00:08:33.822
dMSA and root key procedures against the
stand-ins: seventeen pass, none fail.

129
00:08:33.822 --> 00:08:36.460
Step three lists what each task runs.

130
00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:41.626
The read task, kerberos island, runs
only the directory Get cmdlets:

131
00:08:41.626 --> 00:08:46.885
computers, domain controllers,
objects, service accounts and users.

132
00:08:46.885 --> 00:08:51.328
The write task, kerberos apply, adds
the cmdlets that create and set a

133
00:08:51.328 --> 00:08:56.010
service account, start and complete
a migration, and disable an account.

134
00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:01.285
Notice what's missing from both lists: no
cmdlet that removes or undoes anything.

135
00:09:01.285 --> 00:09:04.880
The output on the slide is
trimmed and wrapped to fit.

136
00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:12.255
Step four checks the Kerberos
accounts and builds the plan.

137
00:09:12.255 --> 00:09:15.655
Nine accounts are in scope,
and the root key is ready.

138
00:09:15.655 --> 00:09:19.617
There are six findings, one
warning and two information items.

139
00:09:19.617 --> 00:09:23.107
The plan has nine actions,
four of them writes:

140
00:09:23.107 --> 00:09:28.105
one completed migration, two new G
M S A's, and one retriever change.

141
00:09:28.105 --> 00:09:29.280
Nothing is sent.

142
00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.620
The island list holds five accounts,

143
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.755
three of them ready for P four point
six, so the hand-off isn't ready.

144
00:09:35.755 --> 00:09:39.393
The reconciliation leaves
one account unreconciled.

145
00:09:39.393 --> 00:09:43.354
And the plan's hash is the same
on every run with these inputs.

146
00:09:43.354 --> 00:09:46.130
Step five reads the nine findings.

147
00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:49.619
N P I K R B oh four blocks
svc webfront's D M S A,

148
00:09:49.619 --> 00:09:54.955
because the account is trusted
for unconstrained delegation.

149
00:09:54.955 --> 00:09:58.078
Oh five and oh six flag svc intranet:

150
00:09:58.078 --> 00:10:01.955
no S P N owner, and an end
date after D oh eight's.

151
00:10:01.955 --> 00:10:05.880
And oh eight warns that gmsa
ops is in Domain Admins.

152
00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.342
Step six reads the plan.

153
00:10:11.342 --> 00:10:14.130
Nine actions, and these
are the four writes.

154
00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.293
Set retrievers for gmsa web.

155
00:10:16.293 --> 00:10:19.456
Create a G M S A for the managed service

156
00:10:19.456 --> 00:10:23.231
account msa print oh one,
and for svc intranet.

157
00:10:23.231 --> 00:10:26.928
And complete the D M S A
migration for svc h r sync,

158
00:10:26.928 --> 00:10:32.617
whose migration started forty days
ago, past the thirty-day parallel run.

159
00:10:32.617 --> 00:10:34.743
svc webfront is blocked.

160
00:10:34.743 --> 00:10:38.381
The other actions are recorded with
their reasons and write nothing.

161
00:10:38.381 --> 00:10:42.105
Step seven reads the island
list and the reconciliation.

162
00:10:42.105 --> 00:10:45.967
Five island rows, each under the
name the account will end up with.

163
00:10:45.967 --> 00:10:51.152
gmsa intranet has no S P N owner and
ends at the end of 2028, so it isn't

164
00:10:51.152 --> 00:10:57.105
ready for P four point six, and its
reconciliation outcome is unreconciled.

165
00:10:57.105 --> 00:11:02.440
gmsa web isn't ready either, because
its end date runs past D oh eight's.

166
00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.902
Step eight is the write path's dry run.

167
00:11:08.902 --> 00:11:12.336
You take the plan's hash, which
in a real run a second person

168
00:11:12.336 --> 00:11:15.916
would review and pass to you,
and give it to the action script.

169
00:11:15.916 --> 00:11:20.727
It reports four would-send lines, one
for each write, and sends nothing.

170
00:11:20.727 --> 00:11:25.040
Step nine runs the same command with
the apply switch, against the fake.

171
00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:30.390
Four writes are sent, including the
completed migration for svc h r sync.

172
00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:34.127
Then you pass a hash of zeros,
a hash nobody approved.

173
00:11:34.127 --> 00:11:37.877
The script refuses: the plan's
hash isn't the approved one,

174
00:11:37.877 --> 00:11:40.403
and it won't run a plan nobody reviewed.

175
00:11:40.403 --> 00:11:44.403
Pasted line by line, your shell
carries on after the refusal.

176
00:11:44.403 --> 00:11:49.428
Step ten runs every test: five
hundred thirteen pass, none fail.

177
00:11:49.428 --> 00:11:51.630
Then delete the output folder.

178
00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:00.251
Lab 2 moves to a test Active
Directory domain, never production,

179
00:12:00.251 --> 00:12:05.966
with at least one Windows Server 2025
domain controller, a Windows Server

180
00:12:05.966 --> 00:12:11.118
2025 member server, an existing K D S
root key, and Chapter 1's Lab 2 run.

181
00:12:11.118 --> 00:12:14.842
Make a traditional test account
and a test managed service account.

182
00:12:14.842 --> 00:12:19.267
Grant the read only the root key's
two date attributes, nothing else.

183
00:12:19.267 --> 00:12:21.534
Run the check, then apply the plan as a

184
00:12:21.534 --> 00:12:24.555
person with Domain Admins
or equivalent rights.

185
00:12:24.555 --> 00:12:30.092
Start the D M S A migration, and complete
it after the thirty-day parallel run.

186
00:12:30.092 --> 00:12:34.855
To back it out, step six runs the undo
command from Microsoft Learn's page on

187
00:12:34.855 --> 00:12:38.446
setting up delegated managed
service accounts, by hand,

188
00:12:38.446 --> 00:12:42.193
because the code never runs it,
re-enables the test account,

189
00:12:42.193 --> 00:12:46.331
removes the test objects by hand,
and deletes the output folders.

190
00:12:46.331 --> 00:12:48.480
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

191
00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.980
The workbook's Chapter 6
has the exact commands.

192
00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:57.117
The federal requirement.

193
00:12:57.117 --> 00:13:01.258
The Kerberos accounts are the
on-premises part of Gate 5,

194
00:13:01.258 --> 00:13:05.668
and the guide's validate step names
I A five, I A nine and A U two.

195
00:13:05.668 --> 00:13:09.730
These are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls.

196
00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:13.368
The module's table maps each
lab step to its evidence.

197
00:13:13.368 --> 00:13:16.857
For authenticator management
and change control,

198
00:13:16.857 --> 00:13:20.180
the plan, with the register's
and each input's hash.

199
00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.455
For account management, the findings.

200
00:13:22.455 --> 00:13:25.930
For least privilege and access
restrictions for change,

201
00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:30.367
the rights each task holds, and the
reviewed list of password retrievers.

202
00:13:30.367 --> 00:13:34.018
For the inventory, the island
list and the reconciliation.

203
00:13:34.018 --> 00:13:37.045
And for change control
and disabling accounts,

204
00:13:37.045 --> 00:13:39.917
the results and manifest
in the apply folder.

205
00:13:39.917 --> 00:13:44.047
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

206
00:13:44.047 --> 00:13:46.674
the agency lines its
evidence up with them,

207
00:13:46.674 --> 00:13:51.080
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

208
00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:56.805
Check yourself with two questions.

209
00:13:56.805 --> 00:13:59.867
First: a Kerberos account
stays on the island.

210
00:13:59.867 --> 00:14:00.993
What must it have?

211
00:14:00.993 --> 00:14:06.543
The answer is b: an S P N owner and an
end date no later than D oh eight's.

212
00:14:06.543 --> 00:14:12.032
That's the program's design guidance for
every account that stays on Kerberos,

213
00:14:12.032 --> 00:14:15.892
and the check enforces it with
N P I K R B oh five and oh six.

214
00:14:15.892 --> 00:14:20.156
Second: why is svc
webfront's D M S A blocked?

215
00:14:20.156 --> 00:14:21.380
The answer is b.

216
00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:24.843
The account is trusted for
unconstrained delegation,

217
00:14:24.843 --> 00:14:29.538
and Microsoft Learn's delegated
managed service account documentation

218
00:14:29.538 --> 00:14:33.155
says that stops working once
a D M S A migration completes.

219
00:14:33.155 --> 00:14:36.570
That's N P I K R B oh four,
and it's why the plan records

220
00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:40.155
the account as blocked
instead of planning a write.

221
00:14:40.155 --> 00:14:42.582
The module page has three more questions,

222
00:14:42.582 --> 00:14:45.430
each linked to the
